  <p>
    Dry Duck is the outlier, not the template. It moved that fast because it was a brand-new brand
    with no legacy problems, built correctly on day one, with an owner who was exceptional at
    collecting reviews. Rewired is the more normal shape: steady compounding over years, with the
    biggest jumps coming when hiring caught up to demand.
  </p>

  <h2>Marketing is a system, not a slot machine</h2>
  <p>
    Local Services Ads, Search ads, your <a href="/services/local-seo">Google Business Profile</a>
    and your <a href="/services/websites">website</a> all feed each other. Someone clicks an ad,
    goes and reads your reviews in the map pack, then calls the number on your profile two days
    later. That job is attributed to nothing in particular.
  </p>
  <p>
    Perfect source tagging is frequently impossible, which is why we care about
    <a href="/services/tracking">booked jobs and revenue against total spend</a> rather than a tidy
    pie chart. An agency that shows you clean channel attribution for a local trade business is
    showing you a simplification, at best.
  </p>

  <h2>Timelines that don't lie to you</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Weeks:</strong> paid ads prove or disprove your economics — assuming the site
      converts. This is why we usually start with cheap paid proof.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>One to two months:</strong> foundation fixes can land fast. Motha Electric went from
      nothing to one to three calls a day
      <a href="/blog/google-category-relevance">in about a week</a>, purely from a corrected
      profile category.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Three to six months:</strong> organic map-pack gains become real and stable, moving
      with review velocity, competition and category relevance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A year plus:</strong> the compounding part, where organic carries volume that would
      cost a fortune in ads.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Month two might be worse than month one</h2>
  <div class="receipt">
    <div class="receipt-label">THE NEW-BRAND CLIFF</div>
    <p>
      Dry Duck launched hot, staffed into the momentum, then watched leads fall off a cliff in month
      two. Organic returned in month three — weaker than the launch spike but real — and climbed
      from there to $200k months.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    A hot first month on a brand-new profile is Google testing you, not a permanent victory. The
    fade that follows is not a permanent ceiling either. Knowing this in advance is worth real
    money, because month two is exactly when owners panic, fire the agency doing it right, or hire
    permanent staff against temporary demand.
  </p>
  <p>
    Similarly: raising your budget 50% overnight can stall a campaign that was working, because
    Google re-enters a learning phase. Smaller steps, timed to capacity you actually have, beat
    panic spend every time.
  </p>

  <h2>Where marketing stops being the constraint</h2>
  <p>
    Most trade companies can grow hard on ads and SEO into the low-to-mid hundreds of thousands per
    month. Then it gets difficult, and not because the marketing stopped working.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Marketing can’t invent trucks or closing skill.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Rewired’s jump to $220k months required going from five crew to twelve. That’s why we run
    <a href="/services/hiring">hiring sprints</a> at all — at a certain point the highest-leverage
    marketing work is filling a van, not buying another click. When the lead flow is healthy and
    the calendar is full, the next bottleneck is people, and another agency pitch deck won’t
    touch it.
  </p>

  <h2>What you should expect from a partner</h2>
  <p>
    Clear stages, no fake guarantees, and honesty about which side the constraint sits on — your
    reviews, follow-up and pricing versus our campaigns, site and profile. You should be told when a
    channel is soft rather than finding out from your own bank balance.
  </p>
  <p>
    If someone promises overnight domination with no work on your side, they’re selling you a story.
    The real version is slower, less dramatic, and considerably more durable.
  </p>
